# Where to put this (copy it, then fill in the values): the user-level file
# ~/.config/nanocosmos/.env (RECOMMENDED — survives plugin updates; same path
# on macOS, Linux and Windows/Git Bash; XDG_CONFIG_HOME is honoured; create it
# with `bash scripts/init_env.sh` from the nanostream-diagnostics skill), or
# scripts/.env next to the scripts, or the skill root as .env, or export the
# variables in your shell. NANOCOSMOS_ENV_FILE=<path> makes the scripts read
# only that file. Never commit a .env.
#
# NOTE: unlike bintu, the Analytics API has no API-key auth. It authenticates
# with the credentials of an Analytics dashboard account
# (https://metrics.nanostream.cloud) and returns a short-lived JWT.
#
# Use a READ-ONLY account. The scripts in this skill only ever issue GET
# requests, but the credential itself may be able to write — the API exposes
# alert-notification write routes (see references/endpoints.md).

# ── Analytics API ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This is the same login as your bintu dashboard account — the two systems
# share one user database. NANOSTREAM_EMAIL/NANOSTREAM_PASSWORD are accepted
# as unified aliases for the same values.
ANALYTICS_EMAIL=
ANALYTICS_PASSWORD=

# Optional alternative to email/password, and the recommended best practice
# for services and automation: an expiring ACCESS TOKEN minted via the Cloud
# Token Service (audience must include `metrics`; max 365 days). The
# nanoStream diagnostics/support skill bundles scripts/create_access_token.sh
# for this; the same token also drives that skill's bintu API scripts.
# Takes precedence over ANALYTICS_EMAIL/ANALYTICS_PASSWORD.
#NANO_API_TOKEN=

# Optional: override the API base URL.
# Default: https://metrics-api.nanostream.cloud
# The old host (metrics.nanocosmos.de, with an /api prefix) was retired in
# API v2.34.2 — do not point this at it.
#ANALYTICS_BASE=https://metrics-api.nanostream.cloud

# Optional: explicit Python 3 interpreter. Only needed if auto-detection picks
# the wrong one. On Windows, `python3` is often the Microsoft Store stub rather
# than a real interpreter — the scripts detect this and fall back to `python`,
# but you can pin it here.
#PYTHON=/c/Python313/python.exe
